# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The emergencyAction function makes several calls to arbitrary addresses with arbitrary data. This opens up the possibility for a reentrancy attack. Although the function uses the require statement to ensure that the call was successful, it does not prevent a malicious contract from calling back into this contract during the execution of the emergencyAction function.

## Tools Used
Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To mitigate the reentrancy vulnerability in the emergencyAction function, you can use a mutex lock. This involves declaring a boolean variable _mutex and setting it to true before making the external call and resetting it to false after the call. Then, in the external contract that might cause the reentrancy, add a require(!_mutex) condition at the start of the function. Here's how you can modify the emergencyAction function:

First, declare a mutex variable at the beginning of your contract:
``solidity
bool private _mutex = false;
``
Then, modify the emergencyAction function as follows:
`solidity
function emergencyAction(Call[] calldata calls)
   external
   payable
   onlyCoreRole(CoreRoles.GOVERNOR)
   returns (bytes[] memory returnData)
{
   require(!_mutex, "Mutex is locked");
   _mutex = true;

   returnData = new bytes[](calls.length);
   for (uint256 i = 0; i < calls.length; i++) {
       address payable target = payable(calls[i].target);
       uint256 value = calls[i].value;
       bytes calldata callData = calls[i].callData;

       (bool success, bytes memory returned) = target.call{value: value}(
           callData
       );
       require(success, "CoreRef: underlying call reverted");
       returnData[i] = returned;
   }

   _mutex = false;
}
`
This modification ensures that the emergencyAction function cannot be reentered while it's still exe

