# Vulnerability details

The rebase token uses the linear interpolation formula:
$$
y = y_1 + \frac{{(x - x_1) \cdot (y_2 - y_1)}}{{x_2 - x_1}}
$$


The `ERC20RebaseDistributor::updateTotalRebasingShares` function is called whenever these functions are called:
```
    /// - enterRebase()
    /// - exitRebase()
    /// - burn() from a rebasing address
    /// - mint() to a rebasing address
    /// - transfer() from a rebasing address
    /// - transfer() to a rebasing address
    /// - transferFrom() from a rebasing address
    /// - transferFrom() to a rebasing address
    /// If the number of shares is updated during the interpolation, the target share 
```

The problem is when tokens are distributed and any users who call function that adds the total supply of rebasing tokens (`totalRebasingShares`) mint within the interpolation period of 30 days, users get more tokens than expected. This is due to the `percentChange` used in the formula where if it's increased , the updated InterpolatedValue `targetValue` will be inconsistent than when it's decreased.

The formula for percent change is:
$$
\text{{percentChange}} = \frac{{\text{{sharesAfter}} \times \text{{START\_REBASING\_SHARE\_PRICE}}}}{{\text{{sharesBefore}}}}
$$

The formula for target new share price is:
$$
\text{{targetNewSharePrice}} = \text{{currentRebasingSharePrice}} + \frac{{\text{{delta}} \times \text{{START\_REBASING\_SHARE\_PRICE}}}}{{\text{{percentChange}}}}
$$

